Dungeon Rooms (CLI Game)

This is a small replayable dungeon game played in the terminal.
The dungeon is a sequence of rooms (Start -> random rooms -> Shop -> Boss -> Exit).


How to run

1) Install dependencies

	pip install -r requirements.txt

2) Run the game

Run as a module from the project root:
	python -m src.app --seed 5 --rooms 6

Arguments:
	--seed  Optional. Sets the RNG seed for reproducible runs.
	--rooms Number of random middle rooms before the Shop/Boss/Exit.


Design patterns used

1) Factory Method (Room creation)

What problem it solves:
	Dungeon generation chooses room types dynamically (random choice). Without a factory, the generator would need to directly know and construct every concrete room class.

Why it is used:
	To centralize object creation and keep dungeon generation simple and extensible.

Where it is used:
	- src/core/dungeon.py
		- RoomFactory.create_room(room_type, rng)
		- generate_dungeon(...) calls the factory to build the room list


2) Observer (Publish/Subscribe via EventBus)

What problem it solves:
	Prevents gameplay code (rooms/player) from depending on console printing directly.
	Without this, output concerns would be mixed into game logic everywhere.

Why it is used:
	To decouple "something happened" (damage, gold, room entered) from "how we react" (printing/logging).
	This also makes it easy to add more observers later.

Where it is used:
	- src/events/event_bus.py
		- EventBus.subscribe(event_type, handler)
		- EventBus.publish(event)
	- src/events/console_logger.py
		- ConsoleLogger subscribes and reacts to events
	- Events are published throughout the game, for example:
		- src/app.py publishes RoomEntered and Message when entering a room
		- src/core/player.py publishes DamageTaken, Healed, GoldGained, ItemAcquired, GameLost
		- src/rooms/* publish Message and GameWon depending on outcomes


Supporting patterns (used in a simple way)

3) Registry (Item lookup)

What problem it solves:
	The player inventory stores item ids (strings). We need a consistent way to map an id to an item
	implementation without a long if/elif chain.

Why it is used:
	To centralize item definitions and keep item lookup simple.

Where it is used:
	- src/items/registry.py
		- ITEMS dict
		- get_item_by_name(name)


4) Factory functions (Monster creation)

What problem it solves:
	Rooms need to spawn different monsters without embedding monster construction details everywhere.

Why it is used:
	To keep monster creation small and reusable, and to make it easy to add new monster types.

Where it is used:
	- src/monsters/* (creator functions) and exports in src/monsters/__init__.py
	- Used by src/rooms/monster_room.py and src/rooms/boss_room.py